WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.965
Ownership and borrowing help Rust enforce
clear rules about memory access,

2
00:00:04.045 --> 00:00:05.182
mutation, and lifetime.

3
00:00:05.262 --> 00:00:08.564
In safe Rust, the compiler prevents many
mistakes that can lead to memory

4
00:00:08.644 --> 00:00:09.239
corruption.

5
00:00:09.319 --> 00:00:11.809
It checks that values are not used after
they are moved,

6
00:00:11.889 --> 00:00:14.326
that references do not outlive the data
they point to,

7
00:00:14.406 --> 00:00:16.679
and that mutation follows clear borrowing
rules.

8
00:00:16.759 --> 00:00:20.386
These guarantees are a major reason Rust
is attractive for security-sensitive

9
00:00:20.466 --> 00:00:20.954
software.

10
00:00:21.034 --> 00:00:23.632
Teams still need to understand where the
guarantees end.

11
00:00:23.712 --> 00:00:27.610
The unsafe keyword allows certain
operations that the compiler cannot fully verify.

12
00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.530
Unsafe code is not automatically wrong,

13
00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.992
and some low-level libraries legitimately
need it.

14
00:00:32.072 --> 00:00:34.292
But unsafe code changes the review
expectation.

15
00:00:34.372 --> 00:00:38.107
It asks the human reviewer to confirm
invariants that the compiler cannot prove on

16
00:00:38.187 --> 00:00:38.566
its own.

17
00:00:38.646 --> 00:00:41.678
A healthy Rust security practice keeps
unsafe surface area small,

18
00:00:41.758 --> 00:00:43.544
isolated, justified, and documented.

19
00:00:43.624 --> 00:00:47.035
The code should explain what assumptions
must hold, who owns the boundary,

20
00:00:47.115 --> 00:00:48.685
how callers are expected to use it,

21
00:00:48.765 --> 00:00:51.201
and what tests or reviews support those
assumptions.

22
00:00:51.281 --> 00:00:54.745
Reviewers should be able to tell why
unsafe is needed and why the safe wrapper

23
00:00:54.825 --> 00:00:56.016
around it is trustworthy.

24
00:00:56.096 --> 00:00:59.182
Foreign function interfaces, raw pointers,
global mutable state,

25
00:00:59.262 --> 00:01:01.536
low-level performance code, custom
allocators,

26
00:01:01.616 --> 00:01:04.539
and platform integrations should be
treated as boundary areas.

27
00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:07.921
Those areas may depend on contracts
outside Rust's normal safety model.

28
00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:10.979
Secure teams make those contracts visible,
test them carefully,

29
00:01:11.059 --> 00:01:14.740
and avoid letting unsafe assumptions
spread casually through the application.
